WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.180
Thank you very much, Caitlin,
Bobby, ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.180
I wasn't sure I was
coming to fashion week.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.680
President Levin, Vice President
Lorimer, if I had -- you

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.640
know, all I got was this
little class napkin.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.400
I feel if it were a little
bigger, I'd turn it into

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.980
a doo-rag so I could
feel right at home.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:47.280
[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:57.340
I just went over and said a
word to Dean Brenzel, because

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:01.550
you may have seen he had an
article in the Huffington Post.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:05.400
It said, now if they'd asked
me to give this speech, this

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.580
is what I would have said.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:10.460
It's really good.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:14.820
It's really good.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.780
But if you had done that then
I'd have missed all your hats.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:33.720
How could anybody possibly be
worried about the future of the

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.874
world when it's in your hand?

00:01:35.874 --> 00:01:36.800
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:45.220
I mean anybody with this kind
of judgment and head gear

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:49.720
will have no problem solving
all the other challenges.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.980
Let me say, in all seriousness,
I'm honored to be here.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.780
I congratulate the graduates,
and I want to thank you and

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:04.660
your families, your friends,
the faculty and staff for

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.320
letting me share this day.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:14.720
I am profoundly grateful to
Yale because of the things I

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:21.360
learned, the professors I had,
the friends of a lifetime, the

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:26.160
fact that I still work with a
lot of people from Yale in

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:32.100
public health and endeavors we
have together in Ethiopia

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.310
and in Liberia.

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:37.640
The President of Liberia,
Ellen Johnson-Sirleaf is

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:38.000
here and I thank her.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:44.420
But most of all, I'm grateful
because if I hadn't come here I

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:45.780
never would have met Hilary.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:51.800
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:59.100
So, she's been in Shanghai for
two days at this big world expo

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:02.200
they're having over there, and
she called me last night and

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:06.100
told me she had given this
speech and how much it meant to

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:07.280
her, how much you loved it.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.220
She didn't prepare me for your
sartorial splendor quite as

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:17.780
much as she should have, but
I'm very proud of the work

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.180
she's doing and I'm very
grateful to Yale because I

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.540
would have missed it if
I hadn't come here.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.000
And we've had a remarkable
life together.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:33.780
I say that because we've been
gone from Yale since 1973

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.720
-- that's 37 years, if
my math still works.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:44.860
And yet it seems to me as
if we were here yesterday.

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:48.040
So I thought and
thought and thought.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:52.260
I said how can I be brief,
which I owe you -- you know,

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.140
when you have as good a sense
of humor as you've displayed

00:03:55.140 --> 00:04:00.000
today, you're at least entitled
to a short speech, and still

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.680
say something that
might be helpful.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.400
Here's the best I can do.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:21.300
The world you are going into
that you will shape, should be

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:26.920
the most interesting, exciting,
fulfilling, stunning

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.280
time in human history.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.440
I mean after all, we've torn
down all these barriers of time

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:43.360
and space and people are no
longer confined to where they

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.540
were born, and so America has
become explosively diverse.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:51.980
You might be interested to know
that at our pavilion in

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:57.640
Shanghai, one of the things
that is most emphasized is how

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.520
there's somebody here
from everywhere.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:04.900
I'm trying to get the World Cup
of soccer to come to America in

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:12.780
2018 or 2022, and my main pitch
is this is the only place you

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.740
can go where everybody will
have a home team

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:15.160
cheering squad.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:21.000
It's an amazing thing
and it makes life a

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:22.760
lot more interesting.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:26.900
The internet is amazing.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:28.640
When I became President,
believe it or not -- I know for

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:30.980
a lot of you this is the dark
ages, but it was really just

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:36.120
yesterday -- on January of
1993, January 20th, you know

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:40.040
how many sites there were on
the entire worldwide web? 50.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:41.980
5-0.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:47.020
More than that have been added
since I started talking.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.580
The average cell phone on
the day I took the Oath of

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:54.480
Office weighed five pounds.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:00.960
Now you know somebody like me
with big hands has to have one

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.720
wide enough so that you only
had to redial about one

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:03.660
in every four times.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:07.860
It's a fascinating time.

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:13.300
Look at all these scientific
discoveries that have been

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:18.640
coming out -- the genome was
sequenced first in 2000,

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:21.860
probably the major scientific
advance of the eight years I

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:24.040
served, and I spent a lot
of your family's tax money

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.120
trying to get that done.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.320
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:34.740
But certainly the most amusing,
off-shoot of genome research

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.120
appeared the last couple of
weeks when we learned that

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:43.200
every one of us in our genomic
make up are between 1% and 4%

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:43.780
descended from neanderthals.

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:52.320
And I'm glad all of us made it
because if only the men had

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:53.600
made it, we'd never
hear the end of it.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:58.320
And now we all have an excuse
for every dumb thing we've ever

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.360
done going back to age five.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:00.800
It's great.

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:05.420
I say that but it
is interesting.

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:12.740
It is interesting furthermore
that the genome sequencing's

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:16.280
first profoundly significant
finding was that, from a

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:18.420
genetic point of view, all
human beings are

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:20.980
99.9% the same.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:24.980
Then Craig Ventor's independent
project said, no that's all

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:26.940
wrong, we're only
99.5% the same.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:32.440
Now with three billion units,
4/10 of 1% is significant, but

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:35.220
from a social, political,
philosophical point of

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:37.780
view, it doesn't matter.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:41.960
You just look around this vast
crowd of your classmates, every

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:47.480
single physical difference you
can see is the product of

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:51.420
somewhere between 1/10 and 5/10
of a percent of your

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:52.200
genetic makeup.

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:59.840
And what I want to say is
most of us spend 99% of our

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:02.840
time thinking about that
1/10, the 5/10 of 1%.

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:06.900
You're going to have a lot of
people tell you, and it'll all

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:11.440
be true, how smart you are, how
gifted you are, how fortunate

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:17.300
you've been, how, as our
committee said, if we just give

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:19.180
one of you a lever, you
can move the world.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:21.040
It's all true.

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:28.560
What I want you to take a few
minutes thinking about is the

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:40.000
99.5% of you, because my basic
belief is the only way that you

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:47.220
can make the most of the world
that lies before you, is to

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:50.460
believe that it's interesting
and fascinating and profoundly

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:54.840
important as all of our
diversities are, our common

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:55.280
humanity matters more.

00:08:57.140 --> 00:09:02.200
And that leads us to certain
fundamental conclusion, as does

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:04.940
the fact that our fate has
caught up with the fate of

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:06.600
the planet which we occupy.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.800
I think about this a lot now.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:16.620
I think about what young people
who have more tomorrows than

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:21.540
yesterdays are to make of the
world they have inherited.

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:24.980
It's really quite
extraordinary.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:33.920
I read just this week, we had
this amazing breakthrough in

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:45.320
physics attempting to determine
how life on earth began, and

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:50.580
the results seem to suggest
that subatomic elements of

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:53.300
matter, which normally under
the laws of physics would be

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:56.660
expected to cancel each other
out over and over and over

00:09:56.660 --> 00:09:59.920
again so life could never have
formed in the first place,

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:01.800
didn't because there were
slightly more positive than

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:06.500
negative elements of the most
basic building block of matter.

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:11.680
If that's true, it is
a metaphor for how

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:12.780
you have to live.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:20.220
Thank God and the primordial
slime that positive

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:21.440
outweighed the negative.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:25.940
That's about it, and about
what you have to do.

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:32.860
And I say that because the
world you live in for all

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:36.300
of its joys has three
problems not very much

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:37.480
in evident here today.

00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:44.020
It is too unstable, it is
too unequal, and it is

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:45.620
completely unsustainable.

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:50.300
So that if you want your
children and grandchildren to

00:10:50.300 --> 00:10:55.440
be sitting on this lawn with
their own inevitable choices of

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:03.940
funky hats, you got to deal
with those three things, and

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:10.860
you gotta deal with them as an
integral part of your life, not

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:14.240
something that's over here that
you think about sometimes,

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:19.080
because these three challenges,
that's where your 99.5%

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:22.760
to 99.9% comes in.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:25.840
It doesn't matter how smart you
are, it doesn't matter how

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:29.100
wealthy you grow, you're
going to have to share

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:30.480
that with everyone.

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:34.720
The world is too unequal.

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:39.710
Half the world's people live on
less than $2.00 a day, a

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:42.600
billion on less than $1.00 a
day, a billion people have no

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:45.600
access to clean water, a
billion people go to bed hungry

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:47.940
every night, two and a half
billion people have no access

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:50.300
to sanitation, one in four of
all the people who die on

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:54.290
planet earth this year will die
of AIDS, tuberculosis, Malaria

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:58.060
and infections related to dirty
water. 80% of them will

00:11:58.060 --> 00:11:59.940
be children under five.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:06.120
Those are the killers
of the poor.

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:10.500
And there are no health
networks out there

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:11.180
for many of them.

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:14.520
I work with wonderful people
from Yale, who just took a

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:17.440
picture with me before I came
in, and our Health Access

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:21.100
Initiative in Ethiopia and
Liberia, and Ethiopia, when we

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:25.120
started, the country has 80
million people, 58 million live

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:28.520
in villages of fewer than
1,000, 60,000 villages, there

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:31.200
were 700 clinics in
the whole country.

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:34.800
Now moving toward 17,000.

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:37.960
We get 17,000 built, everybody
will be within a day's

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:39.100
walk of a health care.

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:43.120
These are things that we don't
think about all the time,

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:44.900
but the world is unequal.

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:52.100
You're sitting here getting a
degree from one of the greatest

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:54.640
universities in history,
founded in 1701.

00:12:55.800 --> 00:12:58.200
There are more than 100 million
children today that still never

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:01.780
darkened a schoolhouse door,
and another 100 million who go

00:13:01.780 --> 00:13:03.120
to school but not really,
because they don't have trained

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:05.360
teachers or adequate
learning material.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:11.440
When even one year of schooling
in a poor country adds 10%

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:13.440
a year to learning
capacity for life.

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:20.860
It's an unequal world within
wealthy countries -- most but

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:23.380
not all, the world has
grown more unequal.

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:32.020
The day before the financial
meltdown, 2/3 of American

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:36.720
families after inflation had
lower incomes than they did

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:39.100
the day I left office seven
and a half years earlier.

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:44.750
Median family income dropped
$2,000 while the cost of health

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:46.320
care doubled, the cost of
college after inflation went up

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:50.780
75%, and America fell from
first to tenth in the world for

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:55.200
the first time since World War
II in the percentage of our

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:58.620
young people 25 to 35 that
had four year degrees.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:04.760
Now I think the Bill just
passed by Congress to cut the

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:07.100
cost of student loans, the cost
of repayment, and let all of

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:10.980
you pay it back as a share of
your income is a very good

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:15.500
start, because that means
people can graduate from

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:18.080
college with a degree and still
join Teach For America, still

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:21.520
join the Peace Corps, still
join Americorps, still go out

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:24.340
into rural areas and serve
people, or go halfway

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:24.680
around the world.

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:27.420
This is a very good thing,
but we have to face the

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:30.440
fact that our own country
grew more unequal.

00:14:33.740 --> 00:14:34.720
The world is more unstable.

00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:35.980
It's entirely too unstable.

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:42.520
We deal with the threat of
terror in every country -- in

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:44.920
America, all the way from the
first World Trade Center

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:49.020
bombing in 1993 to this poor
tragic Pakistani man who got

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:52.580
two degrees in America, got his
citizenship, used it to fly

00:14:52.580 --> 00:14:57.800
home to Waziristan and learn
how to make a bomb and tried to

00:14:57.800 --> 00:14:58.420
set it off in Times Square.

00:14:58.420 --> 00:15:02.080
Thank God he didn't learn
his lesson very well, and

00:15:02.080 --> 00:15:04.960
people escaped unharmed.

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:10.720
But it shows you that when you
tear down all the walls and you

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:15.700
can break through all the
barriers of information, that

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:18.060
the same things that empower
you to get access to more

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:22.820
information more quickly than
ever before, could empower

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:24.540
you to build bombs.

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:28.460
It's an unstable world.

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:31.400
The financial crisis started in
America, pretty soon it's all

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:34.440
over Europe, then it hurts
Latin America and Asia.

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:38.420
Now you've got Greece, a very
small part of the European

00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:42.240
union imperiling the whole
enterprise of the common

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:47.180
currency and spooking investors
around the world in every place

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:48.640
that has significant debt.

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:52.300
We have to reduce
the instability.

00:15:55.540 --> 00:15:59.520
And the third thing we have to
recognize is that because of

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:02.640
the way we produce and consume
energy, the world you live in

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:03.660
is totally unsustainable.

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:11.080
Oh, I know the climate change
deniers got a little juice out

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:14.840
of some stolen emails at the
University of East Anglia, but

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:17.400
an independent scientific panel
just reviewed them and said

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:20.280
they confirm what everybody
knows -- the world is warming

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:23.280
at an unsustainable rate that's
going to lead to radical

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:24.540
variations in temperature.

00:16:25.180 --> 00:16:29.820
When we had this huge snowfall
in February, all on the East

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:32.200
coast, all the way down to
Florida, they opened the

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:35.380
Olympics in Canada and it was
so hot up there they were

00:16:35.380 --> 00:16:37.400
afraid they wouldn't be
able to start some of the

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:38.680
outdoor winter sports.

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:44.040
The National Oceanic and
Atmospheric Administration just

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:49.560
released this week its finding
that April was the hottest

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:52.320
April ever recorded.

00:16:55.520 --> 00:17:01.640
Clearly, we have to do
something, and a lot of people

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:03.260
are discouraged because
there was no agreement

00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:04.240
made in Copenhagen.

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:06.400
I'll come back to this, but the
reason there was no agreement

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:11.680
in Copenhagen is simple --
unlike when Al Gore and I tried

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:15.320
to take this issue on, now
nearly everybody accepts the

00:17:15.320 --> 00:17:17.960
fact that climate change is
real and caused by human

00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:19.280
activity and we gotta
do something about it.

00:17:20.820 --> 00:17:24.300
But many people still don't
believe we can do what we

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:26.800
need to do and still
grow the economy.

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:34.840
When I was your age, a little
younger, Martin Luther King

00:17:34.840 --> 00:17:38.160
used to say, used to quote the
great French writer Victor

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:40.920
Hugo, saying there's nothing
so powerful as an idea

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:41.000
whose time has come.

00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:45.330
Today with regard to this
climate change issue, we ought

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:48.240
to say there's nothing more
destructive than an idea whose

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:51.580
time has come and gone and
people just won't give it up.

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:58.180
The truth is that if we change
the way we produced and

00:17:58.180 --> 00:18:00.840
consumed energy in an
intelligent way, it would do

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:04.020
more than anything else we
could do to reduce inequality,

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:06.920
start an economic boom,
stabilize our future, as

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:10.080
well as deal with the
sustainability issue.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:15.860
It is the greatest opportunity
this country has faced since we

00:18:15.860 --> 00:18:17.800
mobilized for World War II,
and this time it can be

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:19.220
entirely constructive.

00:18:19.220 --> 00:18:21.600
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:33.320
And I'm going to make this
point a little more explicitly

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:37.980
in a moment, but one problem we
have in the modern world is we

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:45.340
got access to more information
than ever before, but we don't

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:47.020
all listen to the
same information.

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:52.820
America's a much more tolerant
country today in most

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:53.540
conventional ways.

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:56.980
It's not as racist as it used
to be, there's not the

00:18:56.980 --> 00:18:59.800
religious prejudices as used to
be, it's not as sexist as it

00:18:59.800 --> 00:19:01.350
used to be, it's not as
homophobic as it used to be

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:03.140
-- we're getting there.

00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:06.240
The only place where we're
bigoted now is we only want

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:07.140
to be around people
who agree with us.

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:10.300
You think about it.

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:14.540
And in our media habits, we go
to the television stuff, we go

00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:17.340
to the radio talk shows, we
go to the blog sites

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:18.380
that agree with us.

00:19:20.820 --> 00:19:23.140
And it can have very
bizarre consequences.

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:28.180
Hawaii, the State where
President Obama was born, has

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:32.080
done everything they can to
debunk this myth that he

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:34.540
wasn't born in America.

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:37.920
They've done everything but
blow up his birth certificate,

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:40.120
put it in neon lights and hang
it on the dome of the Capital.

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:45.980
But 45% of registered
Republicans still believe

00:19:45.980 --> 00:19:46.820
that he is serving
unconstitutionally.

00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:48.060
Why?

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:50.980
Because they've been told
that by the only place they

00:19:50.980 --> 00:19:52.640
go to get information.

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:58.080
I force myself to listen to
people who disagree with me,

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:01.240
and to try to get into
a fact-based mode.

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:07.900
So I will say again, I think
that this is an enormous

00:20:07.900 --> 00:20:14.580
opportunity for you, but you
have to understand just about

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:17.020
anything you think is wrong
with the world can be

00:20:17.020 --> 00:20:21.320
categorized as a result of
too much inequality, too

00:20:21.320 --> 00:20:22.240
much instability, or too
much unsustainability.

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:30.820
So the mission of every
citizen, not just in the United

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:35.140
States, but every empowered
person in the world in this

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:38.780
time has to be to build up the
positive and reduce the

00:20:38.780 --> 00:20:40.680
negative forces of
our interdependence.

00:20:42.140 --> 00:20:45.620
Whenever anybody asks me,
what's your position on x, y or

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:48.340
z, I have this little filter
that automatically runs the

00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:52.000
question through and I ask
myself will it build up the

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:53.860
positive and reduce the
negative forces of

00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:55.200
our interdependence?

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:56.620
If it will, I'm for it.

00:20:56.620 --> 00:20:59.760
If it won't, I'm against it.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:03.640
And I think it's really
important to think about that.

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:07.920
Now let's talk about
what that means.

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:16.120
It means that we have to be
relentlessly committed to

00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:18.180
change, and change is hard.

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:25.060
We once had a member of
Congress when I served as

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:27.160
president who used to say,
you know what they say

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:29.260
about change, let's
do it, you go first.

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:34.100
It's hard.

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:39.740
First you have to have a
vision of the future.

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:43.960
We've got to put America, and
increasingly the world, more

00:21:43.960 --> 00:21:45.640
determinedly in the
future business.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:52.640
Secondly, we have to ask
the right questions

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:53.420
and answer them.

00:21:55.140 --> 00:21:56.840
Most the time I was
in politics, we

00:21:56.840 --> 00:21:58.460
debated two things.

00:21:58.460 --> 00:22:01.500
If you looked at the news or
read the press, usually people

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:03.120
talk about two things.

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:07.000
One reason I combed the blogs
is that they go beyond that.

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:10.360
But most discussion is what are
you going to do and how much

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:12.180
money are you going
to spend on it.

00:22:12.180 --> 00:22:13.160
You agree?

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:16.280
We're going to do something in
health care, how much will it

00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:18.280
cost -- no, no, you should cut
taxes, how much will

00:22:18.280 --> 00:22:19.760
you spend, right.

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:24.680
There's almost no discussion
about the third question, which

00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:27.760
I predict to you will be the
most important question, public

00:22:27.760 --> 00:22:33.860
question, of your next 20
years, which is whatever you're

00:22:33.860 --> 00:22:36.460
going to do and however much
money you have or don't have,

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:39.820
how do you propose to do it,
so you can turn your good

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:43.400
intentions into real changes
in other people's lives.

00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:48.160
The how question will
determine how well we

00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:49.020
move into the future.

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:57.400
And the last point I want to
make about that is that when

00:22:57.400 --> 00:23:03.740
you're determining how to do
something, your goal should

00:23:03.740 --> 00:23:11.040
be what in game theory is
called a non-zero sum game.

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:14.780
One of the most influential
books I've read since I

00:23:14.780 --> 00:23:17.940
left the White House is
Robert Right's Nonzero.

00:23:19.940 --> 00:23:22.920
A zero-sum game, as all of you
know, is the Yale-Harvard

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:24.820
football game, right.

00:23:25.480 --> 00:23:27.140
I mean there's gotta be
a winner and a loser.

00:23:27.600 --> 00:23:33.340
We now in college football make
people play 50-11 overtimes

00:23:33.340 --> 00:23:35.980
until somebody drops, if
necessary, until there is

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:37.520
a winner and a loser.

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:40.880
We're in the pro basketball
championships -- fascinating

00:23:40.880 --> 00:23:43.880
time -- they'll play as many
overtimes as they have to until

00:23:43.880 --> 00:23:46.640
somebody wins, and you know
somebody won because

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:47.360
somebody lost.

00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:50.680
A non-zero sum game is where
both parties can win.

00:23:51.680 --> 00:23:54.080
Zero-sum games are more fun on
the playing fields -- they

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:56.540
don't work in the 21st century.

00:23:56.540 --> 00:23:59.560
If the world is interdependent
and too unequal, too unstable,

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:03.980
too unsustainable, obviously,
if you wanted to change, you

00:24:03.980 --> 00:24:06.520
have to find a way for
everybody to win.

00:24:08.480 --> 00:24:14.100
And that means politics is
important, that means what you

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:18.840
do for a living is important,
and how you do it is important.

00:24:23.360 --> 00:24:23.840
Think of this.

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:27.940
Throughout most of human
history the vast mass of

00:24:27.940 --> 00:24:32.180
humanity didn't have a
thousandths of the choices

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:33.640
you have before you today.

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:36.740
People didn't have any choice
about what they did for a

00:24:36.740 --> 00:24:40.960
living -- they worked to eat
and support their families and

00:24:40.960 --> 00:24:43.520
have shelter and keep people
alive, and all over the

00:24:43.520 --> 00:24:46.980
world today most people
still do it that way.

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:49.300
You have choices.

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:54.520
And as you make those choices,
you should do what makes you

00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:57.960
happy -- most people are
happiest doing what

00:24:57.960 --> 00:24:58.440
they're best at.

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:03.000
But you should relentlessly,
relentlessly, every single day

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:06.800
check yourself and say, am I
building up the positive and

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:08.860
reducing the negative forces?

00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:12.900
Am I helping to create a world
in which we can all win?

00:25:12.900 --> 00:25:16.040
Am I reducing the inequality,
instability, unsustainability?

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:19.600
Am I building all these
wonderful positive things

00:25:19.600 --> 00:25:22.000
that I have loved
so much in my life?

00:25:24.660 --> 00:25:29.300
And, as I said, that requires
you to be good at work, be

00:25:29.300 --> 00:25:34.340
responsible when you have your
own kids, cast intelligent

00:25:34.340 --> 00:25:36.440
and informed votes.

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:44.600
And it also, in this new
century, requires all of

00:25:44.600 --> 00:25:46.260
us to be part of some
non-governmental movement.

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:51.880
The NGO movement -- which many
of you are already actively

00:25:51.880 --> 00:25:54.680
participating in, in community
service here, around the world

00:25:54.680 --> 00:25:57.500
-- is older than the Republic.

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:02.520
Benjamin Franklin organized the
first volunteer fire department

00:26:02.520 --> 00:26:05.260
in the United States before the
Constitution was ratified.

00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:08.560
We've been doing
this a long time.

00:26:09.180 --> 00:26:12.880
But the whole movement has
been in overdrive for

00:26:12.880 --> 00:26:13.680
the last 12 years.

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:16.760
We have about a million
foundations and 355,000

00:26:16.760 --> 00:26:20.440
religious institutions doing
this work in America -- half of

00:26:20.440 --> 00:26:23.100
the foundations have been
established in the last dozen

00:26:23.100 --> 00:26:27.180
years, and there are parallels
all over the world -- private

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:29.680
citizens doing public good.

00:26:31.560 --> 00:26:34.180
The work we do in our
foundation with Yale is an

00:26:34.180 --> 00:26:37.940
example of what we try to do
all over the world, in energy

00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:41.480
and climate change and
health care and education.

00:26:41.480 --> 00:26:45.260
We try to figure out how to do
things faster, better, at less

00:26:45.260 --> 00:26:49.960
cost, and then get it adopted
either by government or in a

00:26:49.960 --> 00:26:53.680
new business model, so we can
go on and do something else.

00:26:55.420 --> 00:27:01.100
You need to do that, because
you got a good deal out of that

00:27:01.100 --> 00:27:05.520
1/10 to 5/10 of a percent of
your genetic makeup

00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:06.040
that was different.

00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:11.880
No matter how hard you work,
no matter what you had to

00:27:11.880 --> 00:27:15.260
overcome, you're still very
fortunate to be here today.

00:27:16.920 --> 00:27:19.240
You got a good deal,
and you have lots of

00:27:19.240 --> 00:27:21.500
choices going forward.

00:27:22.420 --> 00:27:26.440
Some of those choices should
be to do public good

00:27:26.440 --> 00:27:27.000
as private citizens.

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:42.000
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:45.460
The problems with poor and rich
countries are fundamentally

00:27:45.460 --> 00:27:51.380
different, and your needed
here and around the world.

00:27:53.060 --> 00:27:56.400
The problem with poor people is
they're just is smart as we are

00:27:56.400 --> 00:28:00.000
and they work harder just to
keep body and soul together,

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:05.740
but they don't have systems and
organized structures that give

00:28:05.740 --> 00:28:09.540
predictable consequences when
they exert good efforts.

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:13.160
Just think of just the little
thing you're taking for

00:28:13.160 --> 00:28:14.920
granted here today.

00:28:14.920 --> 00:28:18.100
You'd be shocked if this
microphone went off and you

00:28:18.100 --> 00:28:19.900
couldn't hear a word I'm
saying, or if those

00:28:19.900 --> 00:28:20.600
lights failed.

00:28:23.100 --> 00:28:26.720
You know when you leave here,
if you're hot and dry you can

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:28.040
get a drink of water
and you'll be fine.

00:28:28.040 --> 00:28:30.940
I spent a lot of my life in
places where none of that

00:28:30.940 --> 00:28:31.760
is taken for granted.

00:28:33.620 --> 00:28:36.540
We take things for granted
that other people don't have.

00:28:36.540 --> 00:28:39.380
So, for Haiti, for example, the
work I'm doing now with the UN,

00:28:39.380 --> 00:28:42.980
and we have to build them
systems so that the gifts of

00:28:42.980 --> 00:28:45.580
their people can be manifest at
home and they don't have to

00:28:45.580 --> 00:28:48.080
come to the United States or
Canada or France or someone

00:28:48.080 --> 00:28:50.100
else for people to say boy,
those people are smart and

00:28:50.100 --> 00:28:51.560
gifted and wonderful.

00:28:55.100 --> 00:28:58.880
Less than 2% of the African
American population is Haitian.

00:28:59.540 --> 00:29:03.300
11% of our African American
physicians are Haitians.

00:29:05.580 --> 00:29:07.720
The head of one of the largest
foundations in America's

00:29:07.720 --> 00:29:08.640
a Haitian American.

00:29:09.640 --> 00:29:11.200
Some of the most important
people in the health care

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:13.560
community in New York
City are Haitians.

00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:17.580
The Haitians are rather like
the Palestinians -- they're

00:29:17.580 --> 00:29:21.060
only poor in their own
backyard, and they deserve a

00:29:21.060 --> 00:29:24.960
better deal and a chance to
build a better future for their

00:29:24.960 --> 00:29:32.020
children and I think you can
give it to them. [APPLAUSE]

00:29:32.020 --> 00:29:34.500
But it's important to realize
that the reason that can happen

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:39.060
is there is an enlightened
self-interest in the cache

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:41.060
transfers that all these
wealthier countries and

00:29:41.060 --> 00:29:44.060
multilateral organizations are
going to send to Haiti.

00:29:44.060 --> 00:29:46.460
They're our neighbors -- we
realize our interdependence and

00:29:46.460 --> 00:29:47.880
we want it to be positive.

00:29:50.480 --> 00:29:53.360
But that means we have to
keep getting better, too.

00:29:56.120 --> 00:30:00.220
And the problems of wealthy
countries are just the reverse.

00:30:00.220 --> 00:30:04.640
We have systems, otherwise you
wouldn't be here today, but the

00:30:04.640 --> 00:30:09.620
problem with all systems is
that at some point, going back

00:30:09.620 --> 00:30:14.740
to the Sumerian civilization
8,000 years ago, the people who

00:30:14.740 --> 00:30:18.180
are a part of those systems
acquire a greater interest in

00:30:18.180 --> 00:30:20.240
holding on to their position
then continually advance the

00:30:20.240 --> 00:30:22.980
purpose for which the system
was set up in the first place.

00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:27.820
So you tell me how we get
off spending 17.2% of our

00:30:27.820 --> 00:30:28.960
income on health care.

00:30:28.960 --> 00:30:32.520
No one else spends more than 10
and one-half, and we now have

00:30:32.520 --> 00:30:36.920
40 countries with lower infant
mortality rates than we have,

00:30:36.920 --> 00:30:39.980
and we are ranked 35th in
overall health outcomes.

00:30:40.420 --> 00:30:44.820
And the people who fought the
attempt to reform health care

00:30:44.820 --> 00:30:48.060
and finally provide coverage to
everybody said we were going to

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:49.880
mess up the health care system.

00:30:52.500 --> 00:30:56.320
We spend 30% of our health care
dollars on paperwork, no one

00:30:56.320 --> 00:31:01.260
else spends more than 19 from
all sources -- that's $215

00:31:01.260 --> 00:31:04.600
billion a year, that's twice
what it would take to give

00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:05.680
everybody insurance.

00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:10.380
So we have to be in the reform
business, and we have to do it

00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:12.960
with education, we have to do
it with government, we have to

00:31:12.960 --> 00:31:15.820
do with finance, we have to do
with the financial regulations,

00:31:15.820 --> 00:31:17.240
we have to do with energy.

00:31:17.240 --> 00:31:19.640
And every place we do it
we should ask ourselves

00:31:19.640 --> 00:31:20.320
a simple question.

00:31:20.320 --> 00:31:24.560
What will give us more positive
interdependence and reduce

00:31:24.560 --> 00:31:25.340
the negative interdependence?

00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:31.340
A lot of this fight over the
recent financial transactions

00:31:31.340 --> 00:31:35.400
has, to me, missed the point --
not so much whether it's legal

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:38.920
or not but whether it's legal
or not, does it make us more

00:31:38.920 --> 00:31:41.800
unstable without doing anything
to create more businesses, more

00:31:41.800 --> 00:31:44.460
jobs, more investment,
a broader future?

00:31:44.460 --> 00:31:47.360
If the answer is yes, we
should stop doing it whether

00:31:47.360 --> 00:31:48.500
it's illegal or not.

00:31:49.300 --> 00:31:53.620
You need to put the right
filter on your glasses when

00:31:53.620 --> 00:31:56.320
you look into the future
and ask these questions.

00:31:57.000 --> 00:31:59.080
You need to ask yourself
what you can do about it.

00:32:01.300 --> 00:32:03.620
And let me just like
one final thing.

00:32:08.020 --> 00:32:11.360
I talked about all these
problems, but nobody could

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:15.420
stand where I'm standing and
look at you and be pessimistic

00:32:15.420 --> 00:32:16.260
about the future.

00:32:17.960 --> 00:32:23.280
And I have always believed, the
one thing I have never changed

00:32:23.280 --> 00:32:27.260
my opinion on from when I was
your age, I've always believed

00:32:27.260 --> 00:32:30.320
that cynicism and pessimism
were cop-outs -- they're

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:33.280
an excuse to take a dive.

00:32:33.280 --> 00:32:35.940
They're self-fulfilling
prophecies.

00:32:35.940 --> 00:32:38.160
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:38.160 --> 00:32:44.960
And, for example, people have
been betting against the United

00:32:44.960 --> 00:32:50.240
States since George Washington
took on King George -- you

00:32:50.240 --> 00:32:52.480
should go back and read
some of the things.

00:32:52.480 --> 00:32:55.980
Oh, Washington is nothing more
than a mediocre surveyor who

00:32:55.980 --> 00:32:59.660
lost every battle he was ever
involved in before this.

00:33:00.320 --> 00:33:04.400
He doesn't even have a
good set of false teeth.

00:33:04.400 --> 00:33:06.760
Abraham Lincoln's a baboon --
be better if somebody killed

00:33:06.760 --> 00:33:09.940
him before he could take the
Oath of Office -- an editorial

00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:10.800
in an Illinois newspaper.

00:33:13.060 --> 00:33:14.820
I could go on and on and on.

00:33:16.180 --> 00:33:18.000
Nobody remembers the naysayers.

00:33:19.160 --> 00:33:25.560
In the end, all that endures
are the builders, and in the

00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:28.740
end even the builders are
forgotten and all that endures

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:33.320
are the ripples of what
they built, and that's good

00:33:33.320 --> 00:33:35.320
-- that's a good thing.

00:33:44.320 --> 00:33:47.620
So, go out there
with a happy heart.

00:33:49.360 --> 00:33:51.240
Learn to live with confidence
in the face of all these

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:53.760
changes, and give other people
the courage to live with

00:33:53.760 --> 00:33:55.260
confidence in the
face of change.

00:33:56.660 --> 00:33:58.020
A lot of these whacko things
that are happening in American

00:33:58.020 --> 00:34:01.200
politics today are not really
what they seem, they're just

00:34:01.200 --> 00:34:03.780
people screaming -- stop the
world, I want to get off.

00:34:04.760 --> 00:34:07.080
The problem is you can't stop
it and you can't get off.

00:34:08.860 --> 00:34:14.740
And since we're all stuck, we
better make it better together.
574
00:34:15,12 --&gt; 00:34:15,04
Thank you.

00:34:15.040 --> 00:34:20.040
Good luck, and God
bless you all.

